---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import panel from '../../assets/waytutor-panel.png';
import states from '../../assets/waytutor-states.png';

const storeUrl =
  'https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/waytutor/mijecohpljijnlcagmpdgcckpadfldah';
const mailPremium = 'mailto:privacidade@marcio.run?subject=Test%20Premium';
const mailInstitution = 'mailto:privacidade@marcio.run?subject=Institution';
---

<Base
  title="WayTutor — your college material, read aloud"
  description="Chrome extension that reads your college portal material aloud, keeps your place and hands you a spoken summary when it ends."
>
  <header class="hero">
    <div>
      <p class="hero__brow">Chrome extension · Beta</p>
      <h1>Your college material, read aloud, without losing your place.</h1>
      <p class="hero__lede">
        The extension reads the portal topic paragraph by paragraph, remembers where
        you stopped and hands you a spoken summary in the end. Follow along on the text
        or just listen on the way.
      </p>

      <div class="hero__actions">
        <a href={storeUrl} class="button button--primary">Add to Chrome</a>
        <a href="#plans" class="button button--secondary">See plans</a>
      </div>

      <p class="product-meta"><strong>Platform:</strong> Chrome 116+ (side panel)</p>
      <p class="product-meta"><strong>Portal:</strong> Brightspace HTML topics (Católica)</p>
      <p class="product-meta"><strong>Version:</strong> 0.1.0 on the Chrome Web Store, Nov 6 2026</p>
    </div>

    <div class="hero__media">
      <Image
        src={panel}
        widths={[390, 780, 1170]}
        format="webp"
        alt="The WayTutor panel reading a topic: a capybara professor beside a banker's lamp and a stack of books, progress shown on a pocket watch."
        loading="eager"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section split">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      Studying through your college portal means reading long text on a screen that was
      not made for reading. Material piles up, topics stack, and reading gets harder at
      night. WayTutor came out of a real routine: turn the topic into a read-aloud
      session without losing your place and without turning studying into an audiobook
      player.
    </p>
    <p>
      It opens in the Chrome side panel, next to the text. You can follow the words or
      just listen.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section split">
    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <div class="how">
      <div>
        <h3>Extracts the topic text</h3>
        <p>
          The panel finds the content inside the portal viewer and skips menus, credits,
          references and boilerplate. Lists become a single Passage; tables are read row
          by row. The title comes from the topic, not from the first heading on the page.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Reads by passage, with a real voice</h3>
        <p>
          Each paragraph becomes a Passage and one voice call. Audio is cached and the
          next two passages are prefetched, so reading never stalls. Favorite voices
          stay at the top of the picker.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Pauses at figures</h3>
        <p>
          On a figure, WayTutor goes quiet for a few seconds, 0 to 10, configurable, so
          you can look at the image before reading continues. No announcement, no
          description, no caption read aloud.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Picks up where you stopped</h3>
        <p>
          The listening session is saved per material, even after closing the panel or
          waiting. Switching topics resets the state, but coming back to the same
          material returns you to the passage you were on.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>A summary in the end</h3>
        <p>
          When the reading ends, the panel generates a spoken summary of 2 to 4 minutes,
          anchored only in what the material says. You can also jump straight to the
          summary without synthesizing the earlier passages.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section split">
    <h2>The Study</h2>
    <p>
      The panel is a study desk at night: a capybara professor, a brass banker's lamp,
      a stack of books and a pocket watch. The watch is progress. The lamp is state:
      lit while reading, warming up while preparing, low when paused or failing. The
      capybara blinks, tilts its head when you pause and opens its eyes when something
      breaks. Nothing covers the text or fights for attention.
    </p>
    <figure class="states">
      <Image
        src={states}
        widths={[1024, 2064]}
        format="webp"
        alt="Four panel states: reading, preparing, paused and error, each with the capybara and the lamp at a different light intensity."
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>Reading, preparing, paused and error. The lamp does not move: the light does.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="plans">
    <h2>Plans</h2>
    <p>
      Launch prices, in Brazilian reais (BRL). Approved accounts stay on the beta free
      of charge. Premium goes live once billing ships in the app; you will get an email
      when you ask for it.
    </p>

    <div class="plans__grid">
      <article class="plan">
        <h3>Free</h3>
        <p class="plan__price">R$ 0<small>/month</small></p>
        <p class="plan__pitch">To get to know it.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Read aloud with the Chrome voice, no minute limit</li>
          <li>Saved position, highlights and reading speed</li>
          <li>2 spoken summaries per month</li>
        </ul>
        <a href={storeUrl} class="button button--secondary">Add to Chrome</a>
        <p class="plan__trigger">Install now: you join the beta clearance queue.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="plan plan--recommended">
        <h3>Premium <span class="plan__flag">Recommended</span></h3>
        <p class="plan__price">R$ 29<small>/month</small></p>
        <p class="plan__pitch"><strong>R$ 279/year</strong> (20% off) · For the semester.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>90 minutes of professor voice per month, about 112 min at 1.25×</li>
          <li>10 spoken summaries</li>
          <li>6 Gemini voices</li>
        </ul>
        <a href={mailPremium} class="button button--primary">I want to try Premium</a>
        <p class="plan__trigger">7-day trial, no card. Goes live with the first cohort.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="plan">
        <h3>Institution</h3>
        <p class="plan__price">R$ 18<small>/student/month</small></p>
        <p class="plan__pitch">For the class.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Class-wide default voice: course material is generated once and serves everyone</li>
          <li>90 minutes of premium voice per student per month</li>
          <li>Minimum of 30 students</li>
        </ul>
        <a href={mailInstitution} class="button button--secondary">Talk to us</a>
      </article>
    </div>

    <p class="plans__note">
      Paid plans bill in Pix. You can cancel any time: access runs to the end of the
      paid period and nothing is lost.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="install">
    <h2>Install on Chrome</h2>
    <p>
      The extension is published on the Chrome Web Store as an unlisted item: it does
      not show up in search and only installs through this link.
    </p>
    <ol class="install">
      <li>
        <div>
          <strong>Add it to Chrome</strong>
          <span>
            Use the button at the top of the page and confirm “Add extension”.
            Updates arrive on their own after that.
          </span>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <strong>Pin WayTutor to the toolbar</strong>
          <span>
            Click the puzzle piece icon, then the pin next to WayTutor.
          </span>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <strong>Sign in with Google</strong>
          <span>
            The panel opens when you click the icon. Sign in and wait for approval:
            access is granted one account at a time.
          </span>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <strong>Open a portal topic</strong>
          <span>
            The panel lists the passages and the watch starts the reading.
          </span>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="section split">
    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      The material text goes to our server, which generates the audio and the summary
      and caches audio so the same passage is never synthesized twice. Your Google
      account password never touches our servers. There are no ads and no usage
      analytics. The details are in the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>, with a
      version in <a href="/privacidade">Portuguese</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section split">
    <h2>FAQ</h2>
    <ul class="faq">
      <li>
        <details>
          <summary>The Chrome voice is bad.</summary>
          <p>
            Yes, it is robotic and misses technical terms. That is what Premium exists
            for. The free voice is honest and it never runs out.
          </p>
        </details>
      </li>
      <li>
        <details>
          <summary>90 minutes is not much.</summary>
          <p>
            That is about 6 topics of 20 min at 1.25×. And reading with the Chrome voice
            is unlimited: the limit only applies to the premium voice.
          </p>
        </details>
      </li>
      <li>
        <details>
          <summary>What if I hit the limit?</summary>
          <p>
            Premium audio stops and reading continues with the Chrome voice. No surprise
            charge, no lockout.
          </p>
        </details>
      </li>
      <li>
        <details>
          <summary>When can I cancel?</summary>
          <p>Any time. Access lasts to the end of the paid period.</p>
        </details>
      </li>
      <li>
        <details>
          <summary>Does it work on my portal?</summary>
          <p>
            It works on HTML material from the college portal. We are validating with
            distance-learning classes: if yours does not open, write to us.
          </p>
        </details>
      </li>
      <li>
        <details>
          <summary>What about my data?</summary>
          <p>
            No audio or material text is stored under your name. The details are in the
            <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>.
          </p>
        </details>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section split">
    <h2>Start with a topic today</h2>
    <p>
      Open any portal topic with the panel on and let the reading run. If a topic does
      not open or the voice trips on a term, write to us: that is how the product gets
      better every week.
    </p>
    <div class="final-cta">
      <a href={storeUrl} class="button button--primary">Add to Chrome</a>
      <a href="#plans" class="button button--secondary">Back to plans</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
